[[Gubernatorial elections]] were held in the [[United States]] on November 3, 2026. These elections took place in 36 states and three territories. The governorships up for election in 2026 were split evenly - 18 Republicans and 18 Democrats. Democrats won 24 of them, while Republicans won 12, as the Democrats achieved turn-overs in Alaska, Georgia, Iowa, Nevada, Ohio and Texas. Republicans successfully defended two governorships in states that [[Kamala Harris]] won in 2024 (New Hampshire and Vermont), while Democrats successfully defended five governorships up for election in states that [[Donald Trump]] won in 2024 (Arizona, Kansas, Michigan, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin).

The previous gubernatorial elections for this group of states took place in [[2022 United States gubernatorial elections|2022]], except in [[New Hampshire]] and [[Vermont]], where governors serve two-year terms and elected their governors in [[2024 United States gubernatorial elections|2024]].

Fifteen of the thirty-six states and two of the three United States territories with elections in this cycle had officially [[Term limits in the United States#Gubernatorial term limits|term-limited]] incumbents.{{Citation needed|date=November 2026}}

The Democrats flipped six state governorships, while the Republicans flipped two in the territories of [[2026 Guam gubernatorial election|Guam]] and the [[2026 Northern Mariana Islands gubernatorial election|Northern Mariana Islands]]. As a result, this was the first time since [[2009 United States gubernatorial elections|2009]] where Democrats won a majority of governorships.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/04/us/democrats-governors.html|title=Democrats will control most governorships, a generational shift|website=The New York Times|date=November 4, 2026|accessdate=November 5, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.axios.com/2026/11/05/2026-midterms-democrats-state-legislatures-trifectas|title=Democrats poised to break GOP's 16-year grip on statehouses|website=Axios|date=November 5, 2026|accessdate=November 5, 2026}}</ref> At 30 governorships after these elections, this represents the most for the Democrats since [[1992 United States gubernatorial elections|1992]].<ref>{{cite AV media |date=November 11, 2026 |title=This GOP wipeout just keeps getting bigger by the day |type=YouTube video |language=English |publisher=CNN |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S25JKr-YDYs |access-date=November 11, 2026}}</ref> Democrats will govern three-quarters of the United States population, a jump from 57 percent prior to the elections.<ref name=NYTHowMuch>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/11/22/us/elections/midterm-elections-democrats-republicans-control.html |title=See How Much Democrats Gained, From Congress to State Races |publisher=The New York Times |last1=Kim |first1=Minho |last2=Khurana |first2=Malika |last3=Shao |first3=Elana |date=November 22, 2026 |accessdate=November 22, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bradner |first1=Eric |title=After blue wave, three-quarters of Americans will be governed by Democrats |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/10/politics/democrats-win-governors-state-legislatures |website=CNN |date=November 10, 2026 |accessdate=November 10, 2026}}</ref> Democrats won the most governorships in a [[:Category:Gubernatorial elections in the United States by year|single election]] since [[1982 United States gubernatorial elections|1982]], when they won 27. 

== Partisan composition ==
Going into the election, there were 26 [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] governors and 24 [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] governors in the United States. The governorships up for election in 2026 were split evenly – 18 Republicans and 18 Democrats. Republicans held two governorships up for election in states that [[Kamala Harris]] won in 2024 (New Hampshire and Vermont), while Democrats held five governorships up for election in states that [[Donald Trump]] won in 2024 (Arizona, Kansas, Michigan, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin).

