Governor [[Mike Dunleavy (politician)|Mike Dunleavy]], a Republican, was re-elected in [[2022 Alaska gubernatorial election|2022]] with 50.3% of the vote. He will be term-limited by the [[Constitution of Alaska]] in 2026 and cannot seek re-election to a third consecutive term.

A [[nonpartisan primary]] was held on August 18, 2026, with the top four candidates by vote count (using [[first past the post]]) advancing to the general election, which will be conducted using [[Instant-runoff voting|instant-runoff ranked-choice voting]]. The advancing candidates are Republicans former state Attorney General [[Treg Taylor]],<ref name="TaylorAK">{{cite news |first1=Josiah |last1=Pike |work=[[Alaska's News Source]] |access-date=September 17, 2025 |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2025/09/17/former-attorney-general-treg-taylor-announces-gubernatorial-campaign/ |date=September 17, 2025 |title=Former Attorney General Treg Taylor announces gubernatorial campaign |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250917165340/https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2025/09/17/former-attorney-general-treg-taylor-announces-gubernatorial-campaign/ |archive-date=September 17, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> businesswoman Bernadette Wilson;<ref name="AKGOP">{{cite news |last1=Brooks |first1=James |title=Third Republican candidate, Bernadette Wilson, enters Alaska governor's race |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2025/05/14/third-republican-candidate-bernadette-wilson-enters-alaska-governors-race/ |access-date=May 14, 2025 |work=Alaska Beacon |date=May 14, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250515005252/https://alaskabeacon.com/2025/05/14/third-republican-candidate-bernadette-wilson-enters-alaska-governors-race/ |archive-date=May 15, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> former [[Anchorage, Alaska|Anchorage]] mayor [[Dave Bronson]];<ref name="BronsonAK">{{cite web |last1=Barnwell |first1=Jack |publisher=[[Fairbanks Daily News-Miner]] |access-date=October 2, 2025 |url=https://www.newsminer.com/news/alaska_news/dave-bronson-former-anchorage-mayor-enters-governors-race/article_5585e6aa-07f1-433b-b298-03c0b86758af.html |date=October 2, 2025 |title=Dave Bronson, former Anchorage mayor, enters governor's race |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251002214812/https://www.newsminer.com/news/alaska_news/dave-bronson-former-anchorage-mayor-enters-governors-race/article_5585e6aa-07f1-433b-b298-03c0b86758af.html |archive-date=October 2, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and Democratic former state representative [[Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins]].<ref name="Claman">{{cite news |last1=Rosen |first1=Yereth |title=Anchorage state senator enters crowded Alaska gubernatorial field |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/briefs/anchorage-state-senator-enters-crowded-alaska-gubernatorial-field/ |access-date=November 10, 2025 |work=Alaska Beacon |date=November 10, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251111015630/https://alaskabeacon.com/briefs/anchorage-state-senator-enters-crowded-alaska-gubernatorial-field/ |archive-date=November 11, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

After initially finishing second in the primary, former state senator [[Tom Begich]] withdrew and endorsed Kreiss-Tomkins, allowing fifth-place finisher Taylor to advance to the general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Samuels |first1=Iris |title=Tom Begich says he will withdraw from Alaska governor's race |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/tom-begich-says-he-will-withdraw-from-alaska-governors-race/ |access-date=August 29, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=August 28, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260829051917/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/tom-begich-says-he-will-withdraw-from-alaska-governors-race/ |archive-date=August 29, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> However, the Alaska Public Offices Commission recommended against certifying Taylor's candidacy after finding that his financial disclosure did not substantially comply with state law.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Samuels |first1=Iris |title=Alaska campaign watchdog says Treg Taylor should not be allowed to run for governor |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/alaska-campaign-watchdog-says-treg-taylor-should-not-be-allowed-to-run-for-governor/ |access-date=August 29, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=August 28, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260829081050/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/alaska-campaign-watchdog-says-treg-taylor-should-not-be-allowed-to-run-for-governor/ |archive-date=August 29, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On August 31, [[Lieutenant Governor of Alaska|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Nancy Dahlstrom]] declined to certify Taylor, temporarily elevating his running mate, Candice English, to the gubernatorial nomination; English subsequently selected Taylor's wife, Jodi Taylor, as her running mate.<ref name="Taylor">{{cite web|url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/31/pressure-grows-for-a-consolidated-gop-field-in-alaska-governors-race-as-deadline-nears/|title=Treg Taylor disqualified from ballot as pressure grows for consolidated governor's race|first=Iris|last=Samuels|date=August 31, 2026|work=[[Anchorage Daily News]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260831200254/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/31/pressure-grows-for-a-consolidated-gop-field-in-alaska-governors-race-as-deadline-nears/|archive-date=August 31, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref>  On September 4, Dahlstrom reversed her decision and announced that Taylor would be certified after a preliminary review by the Alaska Department of Law raised questions about whether the financial disclosure requirements had been applied consistently.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Samuels |first=Iris |title=Treg Taylor is back in Alaska's race for governor after election officials reverse decision |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/09/04/treg-taylor-is-back-in-alaskas-race-for-governor-after-election-officials-reverse-decision/ |access-date=2026-09-05 |website=Anchorage Daily News |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260906021000/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/09/04/treg-taylor-is-back-in-alaskas-race-for-governor-after-election-officials-reverse-decision/ |archive-date=September 6, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Kreiss-Tomkins won the election, receiving more than 50% of the first-choice vote and avoiding a ranked-choice runoff.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stone |first=Eric |date=2026-11-14 |title=Democrat Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins wins race for Alaska governor, replaces a Republican |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/11/14/g-s1-151719/midterms-alaska-governor-kreiss-tomkins |access-date=2026-11-14 |work=NPR |language=en}}</ref>
