Governor [[Katie Hobbs]], a Democrat, was elected in [[2022 Arizona gubernatorial election|2022]] with 50.3% of the vote. Starting with this election cycle, gubernatorial candidates were required to nominate a running mate for the newly established office of [[Lieutenant Governor of Arizona|Lieutenant Governor]]. Hobbs ran for re-election to a second term<ref name="Hobbs">{{cite news |last1=Govidarao |first1=Sejal |title=Democratic Arizona Gov. Hobbs launches reelection bid as her party aims to build power nationally |url=https://apnews.com/article/arizona-governor-katie-hobbs-election-democrat-2b344c58a85ba98e232f4e9d45f41795 |access-date=October 8, 2025 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=October 8, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251008123534/https://apnews.com/article/arizona-governor-katie-hobbs-election-democrat-2b344c58a85ba98e232f4e9d45f41795 |archive-date=October 8, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and was the nominee, as no other candidates successfully filed for the Democratic primary.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=2026 Arizona Primary Candidates|url=https://apps.arizona.vote/electioninfo/Election/68|website=Arizona Secretary of State}}</ref>

[[United States Representative|U.S. Representative]] from Arizona's 5th congressional district [[Andy Biggs]]<ref name="Biggs">{{Cite news |work=[[KJZZ-TV]] |access-date=January 31, 2025 |author=Schutsky, Wayne |date=January 27, 2025 |url=https://www.kjzz.org/politics/2025-01-27/congressman-andy-biggs-officially-enters-2026-arizona-governors-race |title=Congressman Andy Biggs officially enters 2026 Arizona governor's race |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261003013804/https://www.kjzz.org/politics/2025-01-27/congressman-andy-biggs-officially-enters-2026-arizona-governors-race |archive-date=October 3, 2026 |url-status=live }}</ref> defeated U.S. Representative from Arizona's 1st congressional district [[David Schweikert]],<ref name=Schweikert>{{cite news |last1=Barchenger |first1=Stacey |title=Rep. David Schweikert to run for Arizona governor |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2025/09/30/david-schweikert-announces-run-for-arizona-governor/86426620007/ |access-date=September 30, 2025 |work=The Arizona Republic|date=September 30, 2025}}</ref> as well as business owner Ken Miceli<ref name=":1" /> and concrete plumbing contractor Scott Neely<ref name="signals">{{cite news|author=Gersony, Laura|date=January 21, 2024|title=US Rep. Andy Biggs signals he is considering run for Arizona governor in 2026|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2025/01/21/us-rep-andy-biggs-signals-run-az-governor-2026/77854990007/|access-date=January 21, 2023|work=[[The Arizona Republic]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250121200746/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2025/01/21/us-rep-andy-biggs-signals-run-az-governor-2026/77854990007/|archive-date=January 21, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> with 74% of the vote in the Republican primary.

Healthcare entrepreneur Hugh Lytle and Teri Hourihan ran in the No Labels/[[Arizona Independent Party]] primary. Former Democratic state representative [[Leezah Sun]] announced a campaign as an independent, but did not appear on the ballot.<ref name="LeezahSun">{{cite news |last1=Fischer |first1=Howie |title=Former Democratic lawmaker Leezah Sun to run for Arizona governor as independent |url=https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2025/12/23/former-democratic-lawmaker-leezah-sun-to-run-for-arizona-governor-as-independent/ |access-date=December 24, 2025 |work=[[Arizona Capitol Times]] |date=December 23, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251223202217/https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2025/12/23/former-democratic-lawmaker-leezah-sun-to-run-for-arizona-governor-as-independent/ |archive-date=December 23, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://ballotpedia.org/Leezah_Sun|title=Leezah Sun|website=Ballotpedia|access-date=July 21, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260722063730/https://ballotpedia.org/Leezah_Sun|archive-date=July 22, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref>

This was the first gubernatorial election in which a candidate is required to select a [[running mate]], following the passage of Proposition 131 in the [[2022 Arizona elections|2022 elections]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Barchenger |first=Stacey |title=Arizona will elect its first lieutenant governor in 2026. What to know about the role |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2023/09/21/arizona-will-elect-its-first-lieutenant-governor-in-2026-what-to-know/70848159007/ |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=The Arizona Republic |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251208021452/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2023/09/21/arizona-will-elect-its-first-lieutenant-governor-in-2026-what-to-know/70848159007/ |archive-date=December 8, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Hobbs defeated Biggs by 13 points.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-11-03 |title=Arizona Gov. Katie Hobbs wins reelection, defeating GOP election denier Andy Biggs - CBS News |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/arizona-governors-race-katie-hobbs/ |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=www.cbsnews.com |language=en-US}}</ref>
