Democratic Governor [[Gavin Newsom]] was re-elected in [[2022 California gubernatorial election|2022]] with 59.2% of the vote. He is term-limited by the [[Constitution of California|California Constitution]] in 2026, and cannot seek re-election to a third term. More than sixty candidates appeared on the June 2026 primary ballot. Democrat [[Xavier Becerra]],<ref name="beccera">{{cite web |last1=Mason |first1=Melanie |title=Xavier Becerra announces bid for California governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/04/02/xavier-becerra-announces-bid-for-california-governor-00265564 |publisher=[[Politico]] |date=April 2, 2024 |access-date=April 2, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250402132813/https://www.politico.com/news/2025/04/02/xavier-becerra-announces-bid-for-california-governor-00265564 |archive-date=April 2, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> former [[U.S. Secretary of Health and Human Services]] and former [[California Attorney General]], and Republican [[Pundit|political commentator]] [[Steve Hilton]]<ref name="HiltonCA">{{cite news |title=Steve Hilton, former David Cameron adviser, to run for California governor |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/23/steve-hilton-former-david-cameron-adviser-to-run-for-california-governor|work=[[The Guardian]] |date=April 23, 2025}}</ref> advanced to the general election.<ref name=":02">{{Cite web|title=Candidates for June 2, 2026, primary election |url=https://elections.cdn.sos.ca.gov/statewide-elections/2026-primary/governor.pdf|access-date=March 21, 2025|website=State of California Secretary of State|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260328041140/https://elections.cdn.sos.ca.gov/statewide-elections/2026-primary/governor.pdf|archive-date=March 28, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> Becerra won the election.<ref name=Gambino>{{Cite news |last=Gambino |first=Lauren |date=2026-11-04 |title=Xavier Becerra wins California race for governor with vows to resist Trump's agenda |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/nov/03/xavier-becerra-steve-hilton-california-governor |access-date=2026-11-04 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

Other Democratic candidates included [[San Jose, California|San Jose]] [[Mayor of San Jose, California|mayor]] [[Matt Mahan]];<ref name="Mahan">{{Cite web |last=Gardiner |first=Dustin |date=2026-01-29 |title=San Jose Mayor Matt Mahan enters crowded race for California governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/01/29/san-jose-mayor-matt-mahan-runs-for-california-governor-00750168 |access-date=2026-01-29 |website=POLITICO |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260130094612/https://www.politico.com/news/2026/01/29/san-jose-mayor-matt-mahan-runs-for-california-governor-00750168 |archive-date=January 30, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> former congresswoman for California's 47th congressional district [[Katie Porter]];<ref name="porterCA">{{Cite news |access-date=March 11, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |date=March 11, 2025 |last=Mason |first=Melanie |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/03/11/katie-porter-enters-california-governors-race-00223194 |title=Katie Porter enters race to succeed California Gov. Gavin Newsom |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250312050121/https://www.politico.com/news/2025/03/11/katie-porter-enters-california-governors-race-00223194 |archive-date=March 12, 2025 |url-status=live }}</ref> businessman and [[2020 United States presidential election|2020]] presidential candidate [[Tom Steyer]];<ref name="steyer">{{Cite news |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/11/19/tom-steyer-enters-california-governor-race-00657624 |title=Tom Steyer enters California governor's race |work=[[Politico]] |language=en-US |last=White |first=Jeremy B. |date=November 19, 2025 |access-date=November 19, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260518144804/https://www.politico.com/news/2025/11/19/tom-steyer-enters-california-governor-race-00657624 |archive-date=May 18, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[California State Superintendent of Public Instruction|State Superintendent of Public Instruction]] [[Tony Thurmond]];<ref name="thurmondca">{{Cite news |access-date=September 26, 2023 |work=[[KCRA]] |date=September 26, 2023 |last=Flores |first=Hilda |url=https://www.kcra.com/article/state-superintendent-tony-thurmond-officially-announces-run-governor/45322329 |title=State Superintendent Tony Thurmond officially announces run for governor |archive-date=September 26, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230926191821/https://www.kcra.com/article/state-superintendent-tony-thurmond-officially-announces-run-governor/45322329 |url-status=live }}</ref> and former Los Angeles Mayor [[Antonio Villaraigosa]].<ref name="avca">{{cite web |last1=Gans |first1=Jared |title=Former LA Mayor Antonio Villaraigosa again running for California governor |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/4788052-antonio-villaraigosa-run-california-governor/ |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |access-date=July 23, 2024 |date=July 23, 2024 |archive-date=July 23, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240723192527/https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/4788052-antonio-villaraigosa-run-california-governor/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

U.S. representative [[Eric Swalwell]] initially announced a campaign in November 2025, but withdrew and resigned from Congress in April 2026 amid sexual assault allegations, with his name still on the ballot.<ref name="swalwellca">{{cite news |access-date=November 21, 2025 |work=[[New York Times]] |date=November 21, 2025 |last=Rosenhall |first=Laurel |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/21/us/eric-swalwell-california-governor.html |title=A Former Trump Impeachment Manager Will Run for California Governor |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251205062853/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/21/us/eric-swalwell-california-governor.html |archive-date=December 5, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |access-date=April 13, 2026 |work=[[CNBC]] |date=April 13, 2026 |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/13/eric-swalwell-resigns-congress.html?msockid=29cacfc12e3f6f8d392cdb5d2fa66ea0 |title=Rep. Eric Swalwell resigns from U.S. House after sexual misconduct allegations |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260508154930/https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/13/eric-swalwell-resigns-congress.html?msockid=29cacfc12e3f6f8d392cdb5d2fa66ea0 |archive-date=May 8, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Former [[California State Controller|state Controller]] [[Betty Yee]] also withdrew from the race in April 2026 and will remain on the ballot.<ref name="Yee">{{cite web|url=https://www.sfchronicle.com/politics/article/betty-yee-california-governor-2026-17916212.php|title=Former California Controller Betty Yee says she will run to replace Gov. Gavin Newsom in 2026|last=Bollag|first=Sophia|date=April 24, 2023|access-date=April 24, 2023|website=[[San Francisco Chronicle]]|archive-date=April 25, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230425010118/https://www.sfchronicle.com/politics/article/betty-yee-california-governor-2026-17916212.php|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kcra.com/article/california-governor-betty-yee-suspends-campaign/71075349|title=Former State Controller Betty Yee suspends California governor campaign|last=Ayestas|first=Jonathan|date=April 20, 2026|access-date=April 20, 2026|website=KCRA|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260421074910/https://www.kcra.com/article/california-governor-betty-yee-suspends-campaign/71075349|archive-date=April 21, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref>

Former [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]] of the United States and [[2024 United States presidential election|2024]] Democratic nominee for President [[Kamala Harris]] had considered running,<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 8, 2025 |title=Kamala Harris Considering Run for California Governor – The Positive Community |url=https://thepositivecommunity.com/2025/03/08/kamala-harris-considering-run-for-california-governor/ |access-date=March 22, 2025 |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250322210141/https://thepositivecommunity.com/2025/03/08/kamala-harris-considering-run-for-california-governor/ |archive-date=March 22, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> but ultimately decided not to enter the race.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rosenhall |first=Linda |date=July 30, 2025 |title=Kamala Harris Will Not Run for California Governor in 2026 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/07/30/us/politics/harris-california-governor-race.html |access-date=July 30, 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250730191137/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/07/30/us/politics/harris-california-governor-race.html |archive-date=July 30, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Other Republican candidates included [[Riverside County, California|Riverside County]] Sheriff [[Chad Bianco]],<ref name="Bianco">{{cite news |last1=Horseman |first1=Jeff |title=Riverside County Sheriff Chad Bianco announces California governor run in 2026 |url=https://www.sgvtribune.com/2025/02/17/riverside-county-sheriff-chad-bianco-announces-california-governor-run-in-2026/ |access-date=February 17, 2025 |work=San Gabriel Valley Tribune |date=February 17, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250217193211/https://www.sgvtribune.com/2025/02/17/riverside-county-sheriff-chad-bianco-announces-california-governor-run-in-2026/ |archive-date=February 17, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> diplomat Tim Nelson and businessman Leo Zacky.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-03-12 |title=Who's running for CA governor to replace termed-out Gavin Newsom? Here's a look at field - so far |url=https://abc7news.com/post/california-governors-race-running-replace-termed-gavin-newsom-heres-look/16009625/ |access-date=2026-03-22 |website=ABC7 San Francisco |language=en}}</ref>

[[Butch Ware]], the [[Green Party of the United States|Green Party]]'s [[2024 United States presidential election|2024]] vice presidential nominee, announced his candidacy but did not meet the requirements to appear on the primary ballot and instead ran as a [[write-in candidate]].<ref name="Ware">{{Cite press release |url=https://www.gp.org/butch_ware_announces_next_run_for_office |title=Butch Ware, former Green Party VP nominee, announces next run for office |date=November 11, 2024 |access-date=November 13, 2024 |work=Green Party of the United States |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241113023601/https://www.gp.org/butch_ware_announces_next_run_for_office |archive-date=November 13, 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Becerra won the election by over 30 points.<ref name=Gambino/>
