Republican Governor [[Ron DeSantis]] was re-elected in [[2022 Florida gubernatorial election|2022]] with 59.4% of the vote. He is term-limited by the [[Constitution of Florida|Florida Constitution]] in 2026 and cannot seek re-election to a third consecutive term.

[[U.S. representative]] for Florida's 19th congressional district [[Byron Donalds]] announced his candidacy on February 25, 2025, becoming the first major Republican to enter the race.<ref name="Burkett">{{cite news |last1=Scheckner |first1=Jesse |title=Charles Burkett, Surfside's 4-term Mayor, says he's running for Governor |url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/729249-charles-burkett-surfsides-3-time-mayor-says-hes-running-for-governor/ |access-date=March 31, 2025 |work=[[Florida Politics]] |date=March 31, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250331184826/https://floridapolitics.com/archives/729249-charles-burkett-surfsides-3-time-mayor-says-hes-running-for-governor/ |archive-date=March 31, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Paul Renner (politician)|Paul Renner]], former [[List of speakers of the Florida House of Representatives|Speaker of the Florida House of Representatives]], announced his candidacy in September 2025, and Lieutenant Governor [[Jay Collins]] joined the race in January 2026.<ref name="Renner">{{cite web |last1=Dixon |first1=Matt |title=Second big-name Republican jumps into the race to replace Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis |url=https://floridianpress.com/2025/07/paul-renner-told-desantis-he-wants-to-run-for-governor/ |publisher=[[NBC News]] |access-date=3 September 2025 |date=3 September 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250903193927/https://floridianpress.com/2025/07/paul-renner-told-desantis-he-wants-to-run-for-governor/ |archive-date=September 3, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="CollinsFL">{{Cite web |last=Shepard |first=Skyler |date=2026-01-12 |title=Jay Collins, Green Beret turned lieutenant governor, announces run for Florida governor |url=https://cbs12.com/news/local/jay-collins-green-beret-turned-lieutenant-governor-announces-run-for-florida-governor-florida-news-leadership-forged-under-pressure-not-in-soundbites-us-rep-byron-donalds-january-12-2026 |access-date=2026-01-12 |website=WPEC |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260112202815/https://cbs12.com/news/local/jay-collins-green-beret-turned-lieutenant-governor-announces-run-for-florida-governor-florida-news-leadership-forged-under-pressure-not-in-soundbites-us-rep-byron-donalds-january-12-2026 |archive-date=January 12, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Other Republican candidates included entrepreneur [[James Fishback]].<ref name="Fishback">{{cite news |last1=Cai |first1=Sophia |last2=Leonard |first2=Kimberly |title=Pro-DeSantis candidate James Fishback launching bid for Florida governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/11/10/pro-desantis-candidate-james-fishback-launching-bid-for-florida-governor-00645553 |access-date=November 11, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |date=November 10, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251112020154/https://www.politico.com/news/2025/11/10/pro-desantis-candidate-james-fishback-launching-bid-for-florida-governor-00645553 |archive-date=November 12, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Donalds won the nomination with 47.8% of the vote.

Former Republican U.S. representative for [[Florida's 13th congressional district]] [[David Jolly]], a staunch critic of Trump, joined the Democratic Party in 2025 and announced his candidacy June 2025.<ref name="Jolly">{{Cite web |last=Leonard |first=Kimberly |date=June 5, 2025 |title=Well-known Trump critic David Jolly enters race for Florida governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/06/05/david-jolly-enters-race-florida-governor-00386873 |access-date=June 5, 2025 |website=[[Politico]] |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251001014333/https://www.politico.com/news/2025/06/05/david-jolly-enters-race-florida-governor-00386873 |archive-date=October 1, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He won the Democratic nomination with 61.0% of the vote.

Former [[Florida Senate|State Senate Minority Leader]] [[Jason Pizzo]] launched an independent bid for governor in May 2025,<ref name="Pizzo">{{cite news |last1=Fineout |first1=Gary |title=After dramatic Democratic Party exit, Pizzo says he's running for Florida governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/05/09/pizzo-governor-democratic-party-exit-00340240 |access-date=May 9, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |date=May 9, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> while [[Moliere Dimanche]] announced his own independent campaign in July 2024, becoming the first [[Haiti]]an candidate for governor in Florida's history.<ref name="auto">{{cite web | title=Moliere Dimanche | url=https://ballotpedia.org/Moliere_Dimanche | website=Ballotpedia | accessdate=December 31, 2024 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250105231810/https://ballotpedia.org/Moliere_Dimanche | archive-date=January 5, 2025 | url-status=live}}</ref> Pizzo ultimately withdrew from the race.

Donalds won the election by a 50.01%–48.72% margin. Donalds’ victory was historic as Florida's first African American governor and the first elected African American Republican governor in U.S. history. The narrow Republican victory also marked a significant contrast with the party's landslide gubernatorial win in [[2022 Florida gubernatorial election|2022]]. This was the second consecutive Florida gubernatorial election in which the Democratic nominee was a former Republican and the eighth consecutive victory for Republicans.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Welch |first=Catherine |date=2026-11-03 |title=Republican Byron Donalds wins governor's race in Florida |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/11/03/g-s1-151260/florida-governor-donalds-jolly |access-date=2026-11-14 |work=NPR |language=en}}</ref>
