{{See also|2026 Georgia lieutenant gubernatorial election}}
Republican [[Governor of Georgia|Governor]] [[Brian Kemp]] was re-elected in [[2022 Georgia gubernatorial election|2022]] with 53.4% of the vote. He is term-limited by the [[Constitution of Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia Constitution]] in 2026 and cannot seek re-election to a third consecutive term.

[[Georgia Attorney General|Attorney General]] [[Chris Carr (politician)|Chris Carr]],<ref name="CARR2026">{{cite news |last1=Bluestein |first1=Greg |title=Georgia AG Chris Carr launches GOP campaign for governor, kicking off 2026 race |url=https://www.ajc.com/politics/georgia-ag-chris-carr-enters-2026-race-for-governor-kicking-off-race/UUBZXWHT25GHPNBUVO6RBEKPSA/ |access-date=November 21, 2024 |publisher=[[The Atlanta Journal-Constitution]] |date=November 21, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241121173126/https://www.ajc.com/politics/georgia-ag-chris-carr-enters-2026-race-for-governor-kicking-off-race/UUBZXWHT25GHPNBUVO6RBEKPSA/ |archive-date=November 21, 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Lieutenant Governor of Georgia|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Burt Jones]],<ref name="JonesGA">{{Cite news |last=Bluestein |first=Greg |date=July 8, 2025 |title=Burt Jones enters Georgia governor's race with Trump-aligned platform |url=https://www.ajc.com/politics/2025/07/burt-jones-enters-georgia-governors-race-with-trump-aligned-platform/ |access-date=July 8, 2025 |work=[[The Atlanta Journal-Constitution]] |language=English |issn= |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260417002926/https://www.ajc.com/politics/2025/07/burt-jones-enters-georgia-governors-race-with-trump-aligned-platform/ |archive-date=April 17, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Georgia Secretary of State|Secretary of State]] [[Brad Raffensperger]],<ref name="Raffensperger">{{cite news |first1=Joyce |last1=Lupiani |first2=Lindsay |last2=Tuman |work=[[FOX 5 Atlanta]] |access-date=September 17, 2025 |url=https://www.fox5atlanta.com/news/brad-raffensperger-announces-run-georgia-governor |date=September 17, 2025 |title=Brad Raffensperger announces run for Georgia governor |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250917115946/https://www.fox5atlanta.com/news/brad-raffensperger-announces-run-georgia-governor |archive-date=September 17, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and healthcare executive [[Rick Jackson (businessman)|Rick Jackson]]<ref>{{Cite web|last1=Bluestein|first1=Greg|title=Republican Rick Jackson enters Georgia governor's race in surprise move|url=https://www.ajc.com/politics/2026/02/surprise-republican-rick-jackson-enters-georgia-governors-race/|date=February 3, 2026|website=[[The Atlanta Journal-Constitution]]|access-date=June 6, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260428185507/https://www.ajc.com/politics/2026/02/surprise-republican-rick-jackson-enters-georgia-governors-race/|archive-date=April 28, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> all announced bids for the Republican nomination. Jackson and Jones advanced to a runoff that took place on June 16, which Jackson won, thus becoming the Republican nominee.

On the Democratic side, former [[Mayor of Atlanta]] and former director of the [[White House Office of Public Engagement]] [[Keisha Lance Bottoms]]<ref name="Bottoms">{{Cite web |last=Kamisar |first=Ben |date=May 20, 2025 |title=Former Atlanta Mayor Keisha Lance Bottoms launches run for Georgia governor |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/former-atlanta-mayor-keisha-lance-bottoms-launches-run-georgia-governo-rcna207688 |access-date=May 20, 2025 |website=[[NBC News]]}}</ref> became the nominee on May 19 and defeated a crowded field, which included former state senator [[Jason Esteves]],<ref name="Esteves">{{Cite web |last=Williams |first=Dave |date=April 21, 2025 |title=Democratic state Sen. Jason Esteves announces election bid to run for Georgia governor |url=https://www.augustachronicle.com/story/news/state/2025/04/21/state-senator-jason-esteves-announces-election-bid-for-georgia-governor/83193844007/ |access-date=April 21, 2025 |website=[[The Augusta Chronicle]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250421190547/https://www.augustachronicle.com/story/news/state/2025/04/21/state-senator-jason-esteves-announces-election-bid-for-georgia-governor/83193844007/ |archive-date=April 21, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> former [[DeKalb County, Georgia|DeKalb County]] Chief Executive Officer and  [[2010 United States Senate election in Georgia|2010]] nominee for U.S. Senate [[Mike Thurmond]],<ref name="ThurmondGA">{{Cite web |last=Amy |first=Jeff |date=August 6, 2025 |title=Democrat Michael Thurmond launches 2026 run for Georgia governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/mike-thurmond-governor-georgia-democrat-333e82505c2eb7ede947d0de5120f92c |access-date=August 6, 2025 |website=[[Associated Press]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250806101404/https://apnews.com/article/mike-thurmond-governor-georgia-democrat-333e82505c2eb7ede947d0de5120f92c |archive-date=August 6, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and state representative [[Derrick Jackson (politician)|Derrick Jackson]].<ref name="DerrickJackson">{{cite news |last1=Mitchell |first1=Tia |title=State Rep. Derrick Jackson is latest Democrat to launch bid for governor |url=https://www.ajc.com/politics/2025/06/state-rep-derrick-jackson-is-latest-democrat-to-launch-bid-for-governor/ |access-date=June 3, 2025 |work=[[The Atlanta Journal-Constitution]] |date=June 3, 2025 |language=English |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250603125135/https://www.ajc.com/politics/2025/06/state-rep-derrick-jackson-is-latest-democrat-to-launch-bid-for-governor/ |archive-date=June 3, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Former Lieutenant Governor [[Geoff Duncan]], a Republican critic of Donald Trump, switched to the Democratic Party and ran for governor.<ref name="Duncan">{{cite news |last1=Schneider |first1=Elena |title=Former Lt. Gov. Geoff Duncan runs for Georgia governor — as a Democrat |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/09/16/former-lt-gov-geoff-duncan-runs-for-georgia-governor-as-a-democrat-00564859 |access-date=September 16, 2025 |work=[[Politico]] |date=September 16, 2025 |language=English |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250919150817/https://www.politico.com/news/2025/09/16/former-lt-gov-geoff-duncan-runs-for-georgia-governor-as-a-democrat-00564859 |archive-date=September 19, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

This race was expected to be highly competitive due to the state's nearly even partisan lean and Bottoms' perceived poor electability. Most polling showed the two candidates within the margin of error. However, in the midst of a [[2026 United States elections|historic Democratic wave]] nationwide, Keisha Lance Bottoms unexpectedly pulled away on election night and won by a comfortable 8.6% margin, much to her own surprise.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-11-04|title=Keisha Lance Bottoms surprised by lopsided win in Georgia governor's race: "I thought it could be much closer" - CBS News|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/keisha-lance-bottoms-georgia-governor/|access-date=2026-11-07|website=www.cbsnews.com|language=en-US}}</ref> Along with her victory, Democrats won every other statewide office in Georgia on the [[2026 Georgia state elections|same ballot]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Georgia Democrats flip every statewide office, but appear to fall short in the Senate {{!}}|url=https://capitol-beat.org/2026/11/georgia-democrats-flip-every-statewide-office-but-appear-to-fall-short-in-the-senate/|access-date=2026-11-06|website=capitol-beat.org}}</ref> Bottoms is the first woman and first African-American to be elected governor of Georgia. Bottoms will be the first African-American woman to be governor of any U.S. state. She is also the first Democrat to be elected governor of Georgia since [[Roy Barnes]] in [[1998 Georgia gubernatorial election|1998]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Keisha Lance Bottoms wins Georgia governor race; Jackson concedes|url=https://www.fox5atlanta.com/news/bottoms-wins-georgia-governor-race-after-jackson-concedes|access-date=November 4, 2026|publisher=Fox 5 Atlanta|date=November 3, 2026}}</ref>
