{{Ifexist not redirect|2026 Illinois gubernatorial election|{{Main|2026 Illinois gubernatorial election}}}}
Governor [[JB Pritzker]], a Democrat, was re-elected in [[2022 Illinois gubernatorial election|2022]] with 54.9% of the vote and is running for a third term.<ref name=IL2026>{{#invoke:cite|web|title=Candidate Filing Search|url=https://www.elections.il.gov/ElectionOperations/CandidateFilingSearch.aspx?ID=Z2J%2fvYpKX8w%3d|website=Illinois State Board of Elections|access-date=October 27, 2025}}</ref> With Lieutenant Governor [[Juliana Stratton]] seeking Illinois's U.S. Senate seat in [[2026 United States Senate election in Illinois|2026]], Pritzker's running mate is former state representative [[Christian Mitchell]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pritzker Taps Former Deputy Gov. Christian Mitchell as Running Mate |url=https://news.wttw.com/2025/07/01/pritzker-taps-former-deputy-gov-christian-mitchell-running-mate |access-date=2025-09-30 |website=WTTW News |author=Cherone, Heather |date=July 1, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250712170037/https://news.wttw.com/2025/07/01/pritzker-taps-former-deputy-gov-christian-mitchell-running-mate |archive-date=July 12, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Pritzker won the Democratic nomination unopposed.

On the Republican side, former [[Illinois Senate|state senator]] and 2022 Republican nominee for governor [[Darren Bailey]] secured the nomination against former vice president of policy for the [[Illinois Policy Institute]] Ted Dabrowski, [[DuPage County, Illinois|DuPage County]] Sheriff James Mendrick, and real estate developer Rick Heidner.<ref name="IL2026" />

The election was the first gubernatorial rematch in Illinois since [[1986 Illinois gubernatorial election|1986]]. In the general election, Pritzker won with approximately 60% of the vote, an improvement of five percentage points from 2022. This is the first time a Democratic candidate for governor has won 60% or more of the vote in Illinois, as well as the first time [[Sangamon County, Illinois|Sangamon County]] went for the Democratic candidate for governor since [[1964 Illinois gubernatorial election|1964]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Szalinski|first=Ben|last2=Moore|first2=Brenden|date=November 4, 2026|title=Pritzker wins third term as IL governor. Will he run for president?|url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/other/pritzker-wins-third-term-as-il-governor-will-he-run-for-president/ar-AA2gL26Z|access-date=November 6, 2026|work=Belleville News-Democrat|publisher=MSN|at=para. 30}}</ref> and the first time for [[McHenry County, Illinois|McHenry County]] since [[1852 Illinois gubernatorial election|1852]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schmidt|first=Rose|date=November 5, 2026|title=Illinois county votes for Democratic gubernatorial candidate for first time since 1852|url=https://www.nbcchicago.com/news/local/illinois-county-votes-for-democratic-gubernatorial-candidate-for-first-time-since-1852/4013088/|access-date=November 8, 2026|website=NBC Chicago|language=en-US}}</ref> 
