Governor [[Kim Reynolds]], a Republican, was re-elected in [[2022 Iowa gubernatorial election|2022]] with 58.1% of the vote. While she is eligible to run for re-election to a third full term, she has decided not to run.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Crisp |first1=Elizabeth |title=Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds won't seek a third term |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/5244795-iowa-gov-reynolds-announcement/?tbref=hp |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |access-date=April 11, 2025 |date=April 11, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250910063708/https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/5244795-iowa-gov-reynolds-announcement/?tbref=hp |archive-date=September 10, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Republican candidates included state representative [[Eddie Andrews (American politician)|Eddie Andrews]],<ref name="AndrewsIA">{{Cite web |last=Pfannenstiel |first=Brianne |title=Republican State Rep. Eddie Andrews files paperwork to run for Iowa governor in 2026 |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2025/06/03/republican-eddie-andrews-iowa-files-paperwork-to-run-for-governor-in-2026/84007787007/ |access-date=June 3, 2025 |website=The Des Moines Register |language=en-US}}</ref> U.S. Representative for Iowa's 4th congressional district [[Randy Feenstra]],<ref name="CBSNews1">{{cite news|last1=Jacobs|first1=Jennifer|last2=Gomez|first2=Fin|last3=Hudak|first3=Zak|title=Iowa's GOP Rep. Randy Feenstra launches campaign for governor|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/iowa-governors-race-2026-randy-feenstra-rob-sand/|access-date=May 12, 2025|work=[[CBS News]]|date=May 12, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250512180043/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/iowa-governors-race-2026-randy-feenstra-rob-sand/|archive-date=May 12, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> former director of the Iowa Department of Administrative Services Adam Steen,<ref name="Steen">{{cite news |last1=Opsahl |first1=Robin |title=Former Iowa administrator Adam Steen announces GOP campaign for governor |url=https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2025/08/19/former-iowa-administrator-adam-steen-announces-gop-campaign-for-governor/ |access-date=August 22, 2025 |work=[[Iowa Capital Dispatch]] |date=August 19, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250822093905/https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2025/08/19/former-iowa-administrator-adam-steen-announces-gop-campaign-for-governor/ |archive-date=August 22, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> businessman Zach Lahn,<ref>{{Cite web |author=Erin Murphy |date=2026-03-13 |title=Lahn traveling different path in search of Iowa GOP gubernatorial nomination |url=https://www.thegazette.com/news/politics/lahn-traveling-different-path-in-search-of-iowa-gop-gubernatorial-nomination/article_158bda32-fb0c-4c2f-afc2-cf601c79f843.html |access-date=2026-03-26 |website=The Gazette |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260403160634/https://www.thegazette.com/news/politics/lahn-traveling-different-path-in-search-of-iowa-gop-gubernatorial-nomination/article_158bda32-fb0c-4c2f-afc2-cf601c79f843.html |archive-date=April 3, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> and former state representative [[Brad Sherman (Iowa politician)|Brad Sherman]].<ref name="ShermanIA">{{cite news |last1=Pfannenstiel |first1=Brianne |title=Former Republican state representative to challenge Kim Reynolds for Iowa governor seat |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2025/02/17/republican-brad-sherman-to-run-for-iowa-governor-against-incumbent-kim-reynolds-gop/78963619007/ |access-date=February 17, 2025 |work=[[The Des Moines Register]] |date=February 17, 2025}}</ref> Zach Lahn won the nomination on June 2, in what was considered a major upset.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Knowles |first1=Hannah  |last2=Allison |first2=Natalie |title=Trump's pick for Iowa governor loses GOP primary in upset |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/06/03/trumps-pick-iowa-governor-concedes-gop-primary-upset/ |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=June 3, 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

On the Democratic side, [[State Auditor of Iowa|State Auditor]] [[Rob Sand]] declared his candidacy in May 2025 and won the nomination as the only candidate to file by the deadline. He was also the only [[Iowa Democratic Party|Democratic]] statewide officeholder in Iowa.<ref name="SandIA">{{cite news |last1=Tallal |first1=Skylar |title=Iowa Auditor Rob Sand announces 2026 gubernatorial run |url=https://cbs2iowa.com/news/local/iowa-auditor-rob-sand-announces-2026-gubernatorial-run-governor-kim-reynolds-republicans-democrats |access-date=May 12, 2025 |work=KGAN |date=May 12, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> Sand defeated Lahn in the election to become the first Democratic governor in 20 years, the last being [[Chet Culver]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Brummer |first=Shelia |title=Democrat Rob Sand wins Iowa governor's race, ending GOP control of the office |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/11/03/g-s1-151136/democrat-rob-sand-wins-iowa-governors-race-ending-gop-control-of-the-office |access-date=2026-11-04 |work=NPR |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Klavens |first1=Alex |last2=Klavens |first2=Alex |last3=Harmon |first3=Amy |last4=Harmon |first4=Amy |last5=Adelson |first5=Jeff |last6=Adelson |first6=Jeff |last7=Browning |first7=Kellen |last8=Browning |first8=Kellen |last9=Zhang |first9=Christine |date=2026-11-03 |title=Iowa Governor 2026: Live Election Results, Sand vs. Lahn |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/11/03/us/elections/results-iowa-governor.html |access-date=2026-11-04 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ta |first=Linh |date=2026-11-04 |title=Rob Sand turns Iowa's top seat blue |url=https://www.axios.com/2026/11/04/iowa-governor-results-rob-sand-zach-lahn |access-date=2026-11-04 |website=Axios |language=en}}</ref>
