Governor [[Laura Kelly]], a Democrat, was re-elected in [[2022 Kansas gubernatorial election|2022]] with 49.5% of the vote. She will be term limited  by the [[Kansas Constitution]] and cannot seek re-election to a third consecutive term.

State senators [[Ethan Corson]]<ref name="Corson">{{cite web|url=http://www.kslegislature.org/li/b2021_22/members/sen_corson_ethan_1/|title=Senator Ethan Corson|publisher=Kslegislature.org|access-date=January 13, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210114060920/http://www.kslegislature.org/li/b2021_22/members/sen_corson_ethan_1/|archive-date=January 14, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Cindy Holscher]]<ref name="Holscher">{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Sherman |title=Democrat steps forward in race for Kansas governor, focuses on schools and 'kitchen table' issues |url=https://kansasreflector.com/2025/06/12/democrat-steps-forward-in-race-for-kansas-governor-focuses-on-schools-and-kitchen-table-issues/ |access-date=June 12, 2025 |work=[[Kansas Reflector]] |date=June 12, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250612183902/https://kansasreflector.com/2025/06/12/democrat-steps-forward-in-race-for-kansas-governor-focuses-on-schools-and-kitchen-table-issues/ |archive-date=June 12, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> ran for the Democratic nomination, with Corson receiving Governor Kelly's endorsement.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 22, 2025 |title=Gov. Kelly endorses state senator Ethan Corson as he launches gubernatorial campaign |url=https://www.kake.com/home/gov-kelly-endorses-state-senator-ethan-corson-as-he-launches-gubernatorial-campaign/article_2b3eab4d-ea75-4e71-804c-36c0ca54a926.html |access-date=August 15, 2025 |website=KAKE |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250830212516/https://www.kake.com/home/gov-kelly-endorses-state-senator-ethan-corson-as-he-launches-gubernatorial-campaign/article_2b3eab4d-ea75-4e71-804c-36c0ca54a926.html |archive-date=August 30, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Holscher defeated Corson with 48.8% of the vote on August 4.

Candidates for the Republican nomination included State Senate President [[Ty Masterson]], Kansas Insurance Commissioner [[Vicki Schmidt]], Kansas Secretary of State [[Scott Schwab]], and [[Johnson County, Kansas|Johnson County]] Commissioner [[Charlotte O'Hara]].<ref name="Collyer">{{cite news |author=Robinson, Colter |date=May 14, 2025 |work=[[KSNT]] |access-date=May 14, 2025 |url=https://www.ksnt.com/capitol-bureau/dr-jeff-colyer-announces-bid-for-kansas-governor/ |title=Dr. Jeff Colyer announces bid for Kansas governor |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250514163732/https://www.ksnt.com/capitol-bureau/dr-jeff-colyer-announces-bid-for-kansas-governor/ |archive-date=May 14, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Schwab">{{cite news |last1=Robinson |first1=Colter |title=Scott Schwab announces run for Kansas Governor |url=https://www.ksnt.com/news/local-news/scott-schwab-announces-run-for-kansas-governor/ |access-date=January 8, 2025 |work=KSNT |date=January 8, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250108131729/https://www.ksnt.com/news/local-news/scott-schwab-announces-run-for-kansas-governor/ |archive-date=January 8, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="O'Hara">{{cite news |author=Hammill, Roxie |date=March 10, 2025 |work=[[Johnson County Post]] |access-date=March 11, 2025 |url=https://johnsoncountypost.com/2025/03/10/charlotte-ohara-kansas-governor-bid-254803/ |title=Former JoCo commissioner Charlotte O'Hara announces bid for governor |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250311082326/https://johnsoncountypost.com/2025/03/10/charlotte-ohara-kansas-governor-bid-254803/ |archive-date=March 11, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Masterson won the Republican nomination on August 4 with 43.2% of the vote.

Holscher was declared the winner on November 12.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hanna |first=John |date=2026-11-12 |title=Cindy Holscher wins election for Kansas governor, extending Democrats' wave of victories |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/cindy-holscher-wins-election-for-kansas-governor-extending-democrats-wave-of-victories |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=PBS News |language=en-us}}</ref>
