Governor [[Janet Mills]], a Democrat, was re-elected in [[2022 Maine gubernatorial election|2022]] with 55.4% of the vote. She will be term-limited by the [[Maine Constitution]], and cannot seek re-election to a third consecutive term.

Former State House Speaker [[Hannah Pingree]], the daughter of U.S. Representative [[Chellie Pingree]]<ref name="Pingree">{{cite news |last1=Pendharkar |first1=Eesha |title=Hannah Pingree officially launches bid for governor |url=https://mainemorningstar.com/briefs/hannah-pingree-officially-launches-run-for-governor/ |access-date=June 10, 2025 |work=[[Maine Morning Star]] |date=June 10, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250613163845/https://mainemorningstar.com/briefs/hannah-pingree-officially-launches-run-for-governor/ |archive-date=June 13, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> defeated a crowded Democratic field, which included Secretary of State [[Shenna Bellows]];<ref name="Bellows">{{Cite web |last=Kobin |first=Billy |date=March 26, 2025 |title=Maine Secretary of State Shenna Bellows will run for governor in 2026 |url=https://www.bangordailynews.com/2025/03/26/politics/state-politics/maine-secretary-of-state-shenna-bellows-will-run-for-governor-2026-joam40zk0w/ |access-date=March 26, 2025 |website=Bangor Daily News |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250326124401/https://www.bangordailynews.com/2025/03/26/politics/state-politics/maine-secretary-of-state-shenna-bellows-will-run-for-governor-2026-joam40zk0w/ |archive-date=March 26, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> former State Senate President [[Troy Jackson]];<ref name="TroyDale">{{cite news |last1=Hilton |first1=AnnMarie |title=Former Senate President Troy Jackson enters race for governor |url=https://mainemorningstar.com/briefs/former-senate-president-troy-jackson-enters-race-for-governor/ |access-date=May 19, 2025 |work=Maine Morning Star |ref=May 19, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250519154846/https://mainemorningstar.com/briefs/former-senate-president-troy-jackson-enters-race-for-governor/ |archive-date=May 19, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> businessman [[Angus King III]], the son of independent U.S. Senator and former Maine governor [[Angus King]];<ref name="AngusIII">{{Cite web |last=Everett |first=Burgess |date=May 6, 2025 |title=King of Maine? Angus King III launches governor bid |url=https://www.semafor.com/article/05/06/2025/king-of-maine-angus-king-iii-launches-governor-bid |website=Semafor |access-date=May 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250506113753/https://www.semafor.com/article/05/06/2025/king-of-maine-angus-king-iii-launches-governor-bid |archive-date=May 6, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and former [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention]] principal deputy director [[Nirav D. Shah|Dr. Nirav Shah]].<ref name="ShahME">{{cite news |last1=Miller |first1=Kevin |title=Former Maine CDC Director Dr. Nirav Shah Joins Democratic Race for Governor |url=https://www.mainepublic.org/politics/2025-10-20/former-maine-cdc-director-dr-nirav-shah-joins-democratic-race-for-governor |access-date=June 10, 2025 |work=[[Maine Public Broadcasting Network]] |date=October 20, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251020161938/https://www.mainepublic.org/politics/2025-10-20/former-maine-cdc-director-dr-nirav-shah-joins-democratic-race-for-governor |archive-date=October 20, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On the Republican side, former U.S. Assistant Secretary of State [[Robert B. Charles|Robert Charles]]<ref name="CharlesME">{{Cite web |last=Kobin |first=Billy |date=April 15, 2025 |title=He served under George W. Bush. Now he's running for Maine governor. |url=https://www.bangordailynews.com/2025/04/15/politics/elections/maine-election-2026-republican-governor-candidate-bobby-charles/ |access-date=April 15, 2025 |website=Bangor Daily News |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250415162103/https://www.bangordailynews.com/2025/04/15/politics/elections/maine-election-2026-republican-governor-candidate-bobby-charles/ |archive-date=April 15, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> defeated a field which included healthcare executive and member of the Bush family [[Jonathan S. Bush|Jonathan Bush]];<ref name="BushME">{{Cite web |last=Reed |first=Russ |date=2025-10-08 |title=Jonathan Bush announces he's running for governor of Maine |url=https://www.wmtw.com/article/jonathan-bush-maine-governor-campaign-launch/68910077 |access-date=2025-10-08 |website=WMTW |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251008223826/https://www.wmtw.com/article/jonathan-bush-maine-governor-campaign-launch/68910077 |archive-date=October 8, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> real estate businessman David Jones;<ref name="JonesME">{{cite news |last1=Cover |first1=Susan |title=Falmouth Republican launches bid for Maine governor in 2026 |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/me/maine/politics/2025/05/20/falmouth-republican-launches-bid-for-maine-governor-in-2026 |access-date=May 20, 2025 |work=[[Spectrum News]] |date=May 20, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250522234559/https://spectrumlocalnews.com/me/maine/politics/2025/05/20/falmouth-republican-launches-bid-for-maine-governor-in-2026 |archive-date=May 22, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Maine Senate|state senator]] and candidate for governor in [[2002 Maine gubernatorial election|2002]] [[James Libby]];<ref name="LibbyME">{{cite news |last1=St Pierre |first1=Ariana |title=Sen. James Libby running for governor of Maine |url=https://wgme.com/news/local/sen-james-libby-running-for-governor-of-maine-republicans-democrats-shenna-bellows-troy-jackson-angus-king-iii-david-jones-janet-mills |access-date=May 22, 2025 |work=[[WGME-TV]] |date=May 22, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250522104641/https://wgme.com/news/local/sen-james-libby-running-for-governor-of-maine-republicans-democrats-shenna-bellows-troy-jackson-angus-king-iii-david-jones-janet-mills |archive-date=May 22, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> former Maine Senate majority leader and candidate for governor in [[2018 Maine gubernatorial election|2018]] [[Garrett Mason]];<ref name="MasonME">{{Cite web |last=Shepherd |first=Michael |date=2026-01-07 |title=Garrett Mason runs for Maine governor with the help of a top JD Vance ally |url=https://www.bangordailynews.com/2026/01/07/politics/elections/garrett-mason-runs-for-maine-governor-joam40zk0w/ |access-date=2026-01-07 |website=Bangor Daily News |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260107181930/https://www.bangordailynews.com/2026/01/07/politics/elections/garrett-mason-runs-for-maine-governor-joam40zk0w/ |archive-date=January 7, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> entrepreneur Owen McCarthy;<ref name="McCarthyME">{{cite news |title=Businessman Owen McCarthy joins Gubernatorial race |url=https://www.wabi.tv/2025/06/11/businessman-owen-mccarthy-joins-gubernatorial-race/ |access-date=June 11, 2025 |work=[[WABI-TV]] |date=June 11, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250611170932/https://www.wabi.tv/2025/06/11/businessman-owen-mccarthy-joins-gubernatorial-race/ |archive-date=June 11, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> businessman Ben Midgley;<ref name="Midgley">{{cite news |last1=Kobin |first1=Billy |title=Former fitness executive Ben Midgley joins Maine Republican governor's race |url=https://www.bangordailynews.com/2025/08/05/politics/elections/maine-ben-midgley-republican-election-2026-governor-race/ |access-date=August 6, 2025 |work=Bangor Daily News |date=August 5, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250805201659/https://www.bangordailynews.com/2025/08/05/politics/elections/maine-ben-midgley-republican-election-2026-governor-race/ |archive-date=August 5, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and former [[Paris, Maine|Paris]] selectman Robert Wessels.<ref name="Wessels">{{cite web|title=Wessels declares gubernatorial candidacy plans to scooter across state|url=https://www.sunjournal.com/2024/04/25/oxford-hills-man-scootering-across-maine/|publisher=[[Sun Journal (Lewiston, Maine)|Sun Journal]]|access-date=November 17, 2024|date=April 25, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241228005848/https://www.sunjournal.com/2024/04/25/oxford-hills-man-scootering-across-maine/|archive-date=December 28, 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

Pingree and Charles were joined on the ballot by independent state senator [[Rick Bennett (politician)|Rick Bennett]], who left the Republican Party shortly before launching his campaign.<ref name="BennettMaine">{{cite news |title=State Sen. Rick Bennett launches gubernatorial campaign for 2026 |url=https://www.newscentermaine.com/article/news/politics/maine-politics/republican-state-senator-rick-bennet-run-for-maine-governor-2026-independent-candidate/97-575d0eb1-e965-417c-a5b1-217d0ad441f3 |access-date=June 24, 2025 |work=[[WCSH]]|date=June 24, 2025}}</ref> Pingree defeated Charles and Bennett, becoming the first governor since [[Burton M. Cross]], elected in 1952, to succeed a governor from the same party.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-11-14|title=Hannah Pingree wins Maine governor's race|url=https://www.pressherald.com/2026/11/03/hannah-pingree-is-favored-in-the-maine-governors-race-now-results-are-coming-in/|access-date=2026-11-07|website=The Portland Press Herald|language=en-US}}</ref>
