Governor [[Wes Moore]], a Democrat, was elected in [[2022 Maryland gubernatorial election|2022]] with 64.5% of the vote. He is running for re-election to a second term.<ref name="MooreMD">{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |title=Gov. Wes Moore tamps down presidential talk, launches reelection bid |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2025/09/09/wes-moore-reelection-announcement-trump-spat/ |access-date=September 9, 2025 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=September 9, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250909114501/https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2025/09/09/wes-moore-reelection-announcement-trump-spat/ |archive-date=September 9, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Moore easily defeated physician Eric Felber in the Democratic primary.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Elections |url=https://elections.maryland.gov/elections/2026/Primary_candidates/2026_GP_statewide_candidatelist.html |access-date=2026-03-25 |website=elections.maryland.gov |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260219154405/https://elections.maryland.gov/elections/2026/Primary_candidates/2026_GP_statewide_candidatelist.html |archive-date=February 19, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

A number of Republicans are vying for the party's nomination: businessman Carl Brunner,<ref name="MD2024">{{cite web |title=2026 Candidate Listing |url=https://elections.maryland.gov/elections/2026/Primary_candidates/gen_cand_lists_2026_1.html |website=elections.maryland.gov |publisher=Maryland State Board of Elections |access-date=July 30, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250805142106/https://elections.maryland.gov/elections/2026/Primary_candidates/gen_cand_lists_2026_1.html |archive-date=August 5, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> former state delegate and 2022 nominee [[Dan Cox]],<ref name="CoxMD">{{cite news |last1=Marshall |first1=Ryan |title=Cox files to run for Md. governor, with Krop as running mate |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/elections/cox-files-to-run-for-md-governor-with-krop-as-running-mate/article_e93bc106-82b7-510c-bb06-cfd3232c506d.html |access-date=January 30, 2026 |work=[[The Frederick News-Post]] |date=January 30, 2026 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260131004020/https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/elections/cox-files-to-run-for-md-governor-with-krop-as-running-mate/article_e93bc106-82b7-510c-bb06-cfd3232c506d.html |archive-date=January 31, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> businessman [[Ed Hale (businessman)|Ed Hale]],<ref name="HaleGOPMD">{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Ed Hale Sr. to switch parties, run for governor as a Republican |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/ed-hale-republican-governor-maryland-MIPKLZUXPRHKHPZLDO2J4LWGWM/ |access-date=August 20, 2025 |work=The Baltimore Banner |date=August 20, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250820192808/https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/ed-hale-republican-governor-maryland-MIPKLZUXPRHKHPZLDO2J4LWGWM/ |archive-date=August 20, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and John Myrick, a candidate for U.S. Senate in [[2024 United States Senate election in Maryland|2024]], have announced their candidacies for governor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sears |first=Bryan P. |date=May 1, 2025 |title=Early GOP candidate for governor taps former delegate as running mate |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2025/05/01/early-gop-candidate-for-governor-taps-former-delegate-as-running-mate/ |access-date=May 20, 2025 |website=Maryland Matters |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250511092603/https://marylandmatters.org/2025/05/01/early-gop-candidate-for-governor-taps-former-delegate-as-running-mate/ |archive-date=May 11, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Cox became the Republican nominee on June 23, creating the first gubernatorial rematch in the state since [[2010 Maryland gubernatorial election|2010]].

Moore defeated Cox by a 65.1%–30.5% margin.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Condon |first=Christine |date=2026-11-04 |title=Gov. Wes Moore rolls to easy reelection win in rematch with Republican challenger Dan Cox |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2026/11/03/moore-reelection/ |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=Maryland Matters |language=en-US}}</ref>
