{{Main|2026 Minnesota gubernatorial election}}

Governor [[Tim Walz]], a Democrat, was re-elected in [[2022 Minnesota gubernatorial election|2022]] with 52.3% of the vote. He initially announced a campaign for a third term, but later withdrew his candidacy in January 2026.<ref name="Walz26"/> [[Minnesota]] does not have gubernatorial term limits in its [[Minnesota Constitution|Constitution]].<ref name="Walz">{{Cite web |last=Marquez |first=Alexandra |date=September 16, 2025 |title=Minnesota Gov. Tim Walz will run for a third term in 2026 |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/minnesota-gov-tim-walz-will-run-third-term-2026-rcna229383 |access-date=September 16, 2025 |website=[[NBC News]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Since the adoption of four-year terms in 1962,{{efn|via Constitutional referendum in 1958<ref>{{cite web |title=1957 Session Laws |url=https://www.revisor.mn.gov/laws/1957/0/Session+Law/Chapter/813/pdf/ |website=Office of the Revisor of Statutes |access-date=July 24, 2024 |archive-date=August 14, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240814190758/https://www.revisor.mn.gov/laws/1957/0/Session+Law/Chapter/813/pdf/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}} no Minnesota governor has won a third term.

Following Walz's announcement of not seeking re-election, [[United States Senate|U.S. Senator]] [[Amy Klobuchar]] announced a campaign on January 29, 2026.<ref name="Klobuchar">{{cite news |last1=Griffith |first1=Michelle |title=Sen. Klobuchar launches campaign for Minnesota governor |url=https://minnesotareformer.com/2026/01/29/sen-klobuchar-launches-campaign-for-minnesota-governor/ |access-date=January 29, 2026 |work=[[Minnesota Reformer]] |date=January 29, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260129123718/https://minnesotareformer.com/2026/01/29/sen-klobuchar-launches-campaign-for-minnesota-governor/ |archive-date=January 29, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Several other [[Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party|Democratic-Farmer-Labor]] (DFL) candidates filed, none of whom have previously held elected offices in the state. Community organizer Kobey Layne positioned herself as the Progressive candidate and challenged Klobuchar for the DFL's endorsement, but Klobuchar was endorsed in the first ballot alongside her running mate [[Ben Schierer]].<ref name="LayneMN">{{cite news |last1=Griffith |first1=Michelle |title=Klobuchar fights off challenge from left, wins DFL convention nod in bid for governor |url=https://minnesotareformer.com/2026/05/30/klobuchar-fights-off-challenge-from-left-wins-dfl-convention-nod-in-bid-for-governor/ |access-date=3 June 2026 |work=Minnesota Reformer |date=May 30, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260603013305/https://minnesotareformer.com/2026/05/30/klobuchar-fights-off-challenge-from-left-wins-dfl-convention-nod-in-bid-for-governor/ |archive-date=June 3, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Klobuchar then went on to win the Democratic nomination on August 11.

Republican candidates included State House Speaker [[Lisa Demuth]],<ref name="Demuth">{{Cite web |last=Peterson |first=Lindsey |date=2025-11-02 |title=Minnesota House Speaker Lisa Demuth to announce she's entering the Governor's race |url=https://www.audacy.com/wccoradio/news/local/rep-house-speaker-lisa-demuth-will-enter-governors-race |access-date=2025-11-02 |website=Audacy |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251102232642/https://www.audacy.com/wccoradio/news/local/rep-house-speaker-lisa-demuth-will-enter-governors-race |archive-date=November 2, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[My Pillow]] CEO and conservative activist [[Mike Lindell]],<ref name="Lindell">{{cite news |title=MyPillow's Mike Lindell announces he's running for Minnesota governor as a Republican |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/minnesota/news/mike-lindell-minnesota-governor-republican-campaign-mypillow/ |access-date=December 11, 2025 |work=[[CBS Minnesota]] |date=December 11, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251211162044/https://www.cbsnews.com/minnesota/news/mike-lindell-minnesota-governor-republican-campaign-mypillow/ |archive-date=December 11, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and healthcare technology executive Kendall Qualls.<ref name="Qualls">{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Peter |title=Kendall Qualls enters 2026 Minnesota governor's race as a GOP nomination contender |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2025/05/08/qualls-makes-second-run-for-minnesota-governor |access-date=May 8, 2025 |work=[[Minnesota Public Radio]] |date=May 8, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> Qualls received the Republican Party's endorsement after ten rounds of balloting.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Murphy |first1=Esme |title=How Kendall Qualls upset Lisa Demuth for the Minnesota GOP governor endorsement |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/minnesota/news/kendall-qualls-minnesota-gop-governor-endorsement-lisa-demuth/ |access-date=3 June 2026 |work=CBS Minnesota |date=June 1, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260601085140/https://www.cbsnews.com/minnesota/news/kendall-qualls-minnesota-gop-governor-endorsement-lisa-demuth/ |archive-date=June 1, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Demuth went on to win the Republican nomination, defeating both Lindell and Qualls.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ferguson |first=Dana |date=2026-08-11 |title=Trump-backed Mike Lindell loses primary for Minnesota governor to Lisa Demuth |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/08/11/nx-s1-5928394/2026-minnesota-governor-primary-election-lisa-demuth-mike-lindell |access-date=2026-11-07 |work=NPR |language=en}}</ref>

Klobuchar defeated Demuth by 13 points, becoming the first female governor of Minnesota.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Akaolisa|first=Tom|date=2026-08-17|title=MINNEAPOLIMEDIA EDITORIAL {{!}} Minnesota Will Elect Its First Woman Governor. The Harder Task Is Governing a Fractured State {{!}} MinneapoliMedia|url=https://minneapolimedia.town.news/g/coon-rapids-mn/n/384781/minneapolimedia-editorial-minnesota-will-elect-its-first-woman-governor|access-date=2026-08-28|website=minneapolimedia.town.news|language=en}}</ref> Republicans have not won a gubernatorial election in Minnesota since [[2006 Minnesota gubernatorial election|2006]]. Although this was Klobuchar's narrowest victory in any of her statewide campaigns, she won by the largest margin in a Minnesota gubernatorial election since [[1994 Minnesota gubernatorial election|1994]], and by the largest margin for a non-incumbent candidate since [[1982 Minnesota gubernatorial election|1982]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ferguson |first=Dana |date=2026-11-03 |title=Amy Klobuchar, a Minnesota political trailblazer, will be state's first female governor |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2026/11/03/minnesota-voters-set-to-hire-their-next-governor-and-make-a-little-history |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=MPR News |language=en}}</ref>
