{{main|2026 New Hampshire gubernatorial election}}

Governor [[Kelly Ayotte]], a Republican, was elected in [[2024 New Hampshire gubernatorial election|2024]] with 53.6% of the vote. She won renomination for a second two-year term in 2026.

Former [[Executive Council of New Hampshire|executive councilor]] [[Cinde Warmington]], who unsuccessfully sought the Democratic nomination in 2024, won the Democratic nomination.

Businessman Jon Kiper is running as the nominee of the newly formed Community First party.<ref name=KiperCF>{{Cite web |last=Graham |first=Michael |date=2026-04-13 |title=Former Dem Kiper Forming Third Party, Staying in Gov's Race |url=https://nhjournal.com/former-dem-kiper-forming-third-party-staying-in-govs-race/ |access-date=2026-04-14 |website=NH Journal |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260414104223/https://nhjournal.com/former-dem-kiper-forming-third-party-staying-in-govs-race/ |archive-date=April 14, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Ayotte won re-election to a second term by just over four points.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Harris |first=Jackie |date=2026-11-04 |title=Ayotte secures reelection, providing a rare bright spot for NH Republicans |url=https://www.nhpr.org/nh-news/2026-11-04/governor-nh-newhampshire-election-ayotte |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=New Hampshire Public Radio |language=en}}</ref>
