{{See also|2026 Rhode Island lieutenant gubernatorial election}}
Governor [[Dan McKee]], a Democrat, took office in 2021 following the resignation of [[Gina Raimondo]] and was elected to a full term in [[2022 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|2022]] with 57.9% of the vote.<ref name=DanMcKee>{{cite news |last1=Fortier |first1=Marc |title=RI Gov. Dan McKee announces reelection campaign |url=https://www.nbcboston.com/news/local/ri-gov-dan-mckee-announces-reelection-campaign/3646001/ |access-date=March 3, 2025 |work=[[NBC10 Boston]] |date=March 3, 2025}}</ref>

[[Helena Foulkes]], a former [[CVS Pharmacy]] executive, challenged McKee in a rematch of their closely contested 2022 primary.<ref name="Foulkes">{{cite web |last=Anderson |first=Patrick |date=September 9, 2025 |title=Helena Foulkes launches second bid for governor. What we know. |url=https://www.providencejournal.com/story/news/politics/2025/09/09/helena-foulkes-launches-bid-to-unseat-mckee-as-ri-governor-in-2026/86055267007/ |access-date=September 9, 2025 |publisher=[[The Providence Journal]] |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250911034653/https://www.providencejournal.com/story/news/politics/2025/09/09/helena-foulkes-launches-bid-to-unseat-mckee-as-ri-governor-in-2026/86055267007/ |archive-date=September 11, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Foulkes won the nomination, defeating McKee and making him the first elected governor to lose renomination since [[2014 Hawaii gubernatorial election|2014]].{{efn|Governor [[Jeff Colyer]] of Kansas, who had taken office after the resignation of [[Sam Brownback]], was defeated by [[Kris Kobach]] in the [[2018 Kansas gubernatorial election|2018]] primary election.}}

2022 lieutenant governor nominee [[Aaron Guckian]]<ref name="Guckian">{{cite web |last1=Fitzpatrick |first1=Edward |title=Republican Aaron Guckian to run for governor of Rhode Island |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/2025/11/30/metro/ri-republican-aaron-guckian-governor/ |access-date=November 30, 2025 |work=[[The Boston Globe]] |date=November 30, 2025}}</ref> defeated actress Elaine Pelino for the Republican nomination.<ref name="Pelino">{{cite web |last1=Gregg |first1=Katherine |title=These are the wild-card GOP candidates who are running or might run for RI top offices |url=https://www.providencejournal.com/story/news/politics/2025/11/03/ri-republican-candidate-field-in-2026-begins-to-take-shape/86609140007/ |access-date=November 14, 2025 |work=[[Providence Journal]] |date=November 3, 2025 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> Pelino subsequently requested a recount, which was approved by the Rhode Island Board of Elections. The recount did not change the outcome.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-15 |title=Recount does not change outcome of Republican primary in governor's race |url=https://turnto10.com/news/local/officials-begin-recount-of-republican-primary-for-rhode-island-governor-sept-15-2026 |access-date=2026-09-15 |website=WJAR |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915191142/https://turnto10.com/news/local/officials-begin-recount-of-republican-primary-for-rhode-island-governor-sept-15-2026 |archive-date=September 15, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Founder of the [[Moderate Party of Rhode Island]] and businessman [[Ken Block (politician)|Ken Block]] ran as an independent candidate.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Shea |first1=Christopher |title=Ken Block is running for governor again. This time as an independent. |url=https://rhodeislandcurrent.com/2026/04/02/ken-block-is-running-for-governor-again-this-time-as-an-independent/ |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[Rhode Island Current]] |date=2 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260630015111/https://rhodeislandcurrent.com/2026/04/02/ken-block-is-running-for-governor-again-this-time-as-an-independent/ |archive-date=June 30, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Foulkes defeated Block, who finished second, and Guckian, receiving 57.7% of the vote to Block's 37.7% and Guckian's 13.2%.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gregg |first=Katherine |title=Democrat Helena Foulkes wins hard-fought RI governor race, Block concedes |url=https://www.providencejournal.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/11/03/ri-governor-race-results-2026/92465912007/ |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=The Providence Journal |language=en-US}}</ref>
