{{Main|2026 South Dakota gubernatorial election}}

[[Governor of South Dakota|Governor]] [[Larry Rhoden]], a Republican, assumed office in 2025 following the resignation of [[Kristi Noem]] to become [[United States Secretary of Homeland Security|Secretary of Homeland Security]].<ref name="Rhoden">{{cite news |author=C.J. Keene |title=Gov. Larry Rhoden officially announces reelection campaign |url=https://www.sdpb.org/politics/2025-11-18/gov-larry-rhoden-officially-announces-reelection-campaign |access-date=November 18, 2025 |work=[[SDPB]] |date=November 18, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251118204530/https://www.sdpb.org/politics/2025-11-18/gov-larry-rhoden-officially-announces-reelection-campaign |archive-date=November 18, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Noem had been re-elected in [[2022 South Dakota gubernatorial election|2022]] with 62% of the vote. Rhoden ran for a full term.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Keene |first=C. J. |date=2025-11-18 |title=Gov. Larry Rhoden officially announces election campaign |url=https://www.sdpb.org/politics/2025-11-18/gov-larry-rhoden-officially-announces-election-campaign |access-date=2026-03-29 |website=SDPB |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260108110711/https://www.sdpb.org/politics/2025-11-18/gov-larry-rhoden-officially-announces-election-campaign |archive-date=January 8, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Challenging Rhoden in the Republican primary were Speaker of the [[South Dakota House of Representatives]] [[Jon Hansen]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Huber |first=Makenzie |date=2025-04-25 |title=First candidate for governor focuses on property rights, spending and halting 'corporate welfare' |url=https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2025/04/24/first-candidate-governor-race-jon-hansen-focuses-property-rights-spending-cuts-halting-corporate-welfare-karla-lems/ |access-date=2026-03-29 |website=South Dakota Searchlight |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250428135758/https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2025/04/24/first-candidate-governor-race-jon-hansen-focuses-property-rights-spending-cuts-halting-corporate-welfare-karla-lems/ |archive-date=April 28, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> U.S. Representative [[Dusty Johnson]]<ref name="dustyjohnson">{{cite news |title=Saying it's time for a 'new chapter,' Dusty Johnson announces his campaign for governor • South Dakota Searchlight |url=https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2025/06/30/dusty-johnson-makes-it-official-with-announcement-of-campaign-for-governor/ |access-date=June 30, 2025 |work=South Dakota Searchlight |date=June 30, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250701012205/https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2025/06/30/dusty-johnson-makes-it-official-with-announcement-of-campaign-for-governor/ |archive-date=July 1, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and real estate entrepreneur Toby Doeden.<ref name="Doerden">{{cite news |last1=Sneve |first1=Joe |title=Toby Doeden will run in South Dakota's next election for governor |url=https://www.thedakotascout.com/p/toby-doeden-will-run-in-south-dakotas |access-date=May 23, 2025 |work=The Dakota Scout |date=May 23, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250523132039/https://www.thedakotascout.com/p/toby-doeden-will-run-in-south-dakotas |archive-date=May 23, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Doeden and Rhoden advanced to a runoff, which took place on July 28. Rhoden defeated Doeden in a landslide in the runoff and became the nominee.

On the Democratic side, [[South Dakota Democratic Party]] executive director [[Daniel Ahlers]]<ref name="Ahlers">{{cite news |title=Leader of South Dakota Democratic Party announces his campaign for governor |url=https://southdakotasearchlight.com/briefs/leader-of-south-dakota-democratic-party-announces-his-campaign-for-governor/ |access-date=February 9, 2026 |work=South Dakota Searchlight |date=February 9, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260209184224/https://southdakotasearchlight.com/briefs/leader-of-south-dakota-democratic-party-announces-his-campaign-for-governor/ |archive-date=February 9, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> was the nominee.

Rhoden defeated Ahlers by nearly 27 points, winning a full term in office and continuing the longest currently-active gubernatorial winning streak for either party, as Democrats have not won a South Dakota gubernatorial election since [[1974 South Dakota gubernatorial election|1974]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Keene |first=C. J. |date=2026-11-04 |title=Rhoden wins South Dakota gubernatorial race |url=https://www.sdpb.org/elections/2026-11-03/ap-rhoden-wins-south-dakota-gubernatorial-race |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=SDPB |language=en}}</ref>
