{{Main|2026 Tennessee gubernatorial election}}

Republican Governor [[Bill Lee (Tennessee politician)|Bill Lee]] was re-elected in [[2022 Tennessee gubernatorial election|2022]] with 64.9% of the vote. He was term-limited by the [[Constitution of Tennessee|Tennessee Constitution]] in 2026 and could not seek re-election to a third consecutive term.

U.S. Senator [[Marsha Blackburn]],<ref name="BlackburnTN">{{cite news |last1=Taylor |first1=Sarah Grace |title=Marsha Blackburn announces run for Governor of Tennessee |url=https://nashvillebanner.com/2025/08/06/marsha-blackburn-tennessee-governor-campaign-launch/ |access-date=August 6, 2025 |work=Nashville Banner |date=August 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250806123200/https://nashvillebanner.com/2025/08/06/marsha-blackburn-tennessee-governor-campaign-launch/ |archive-date=August 6, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> state representative [[Monty Fritts]],<ref name="FrittsTN">{{cite news |last1=Grace Taylor |first1=Sarah |title=State Rep. Monty Fritts announces 2026 governor run |url=https://nashvillebanner.com/2025/09/08/ascend-amphitheater-opry-entertainment-group/ |access-date=September 8, 2025 |work=Nashville Banner |date=September 8, 2025}}</ref> and U.S. Representative for [[Tennessee's 6th congressional district]] [[John Rose (Tennessee politician)|John Rose]]<ref name="Rose">{{Cite web |title=Rose tells friends, supporters he's on go for 2026 governor's race |url=https://pro.stateaffairs.com/tn/elections/rose-tennessee-governor |access-date=January 27, 2025 |website=State Affairs |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250127184111/https://pro.stateaffairs.com/tn/elections/rose-tennessee-governor |archive-date=January 27, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> ran for the Republican nomination. Blackburn won the nomination with 43.4% of the vote on August 6.

For the Democrats, [[Memphis City Council|Memphis city councilor]] Jerri Green<ref name="GreenTN">{{Cite web |last=Finton |first=Lucas |date=July 14, 2025 |title=Memphis City Councilwoman Jerri Green officially throws hat into Tennessee governor's race |url=https://www.commercialappeal.com/story/news/local/2025/07/14/memphis-city-councilwoman-jerri-tennessee-governor/85191204007/ |access-date=July 17, 2025 |website=[[The Commercial Appeal]] |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250716152431/https://www.commercialappeal.com/story/news/local/2025/07/14/memphis-city-councilwoman-jerri-tennessee-governor/85191204007/ |archive-date=July 16, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> defeated leader of New Chicago Community Development Corporation and 2022 candidate for governor [[Carnita Atwater]], nominee for the 109th district in the [[Illinois House of Representatives]] in [[2010 Illinois House of Representatives election|2010]] Tim Cyr, and guitarist Adam Kurtz for the nomination.<ref name="Contested">{{cite web |last1=Cornell |first1=Josh |date=September 16, 2025 |title=The Civic Scoop: Cornell on the ongoing Tennessee governor's race |url=https://highlandecho.com/the-civic-scoop-cornell-on-the-ongoing-tennessee-governors-race/ |access-date=September 16, 2025 |website=[[The Highland Echo]] |quote=There are also four democrats running for this position: Jerri Green, Carnita Atwater, Adam Kurtz, and Tim Cyr... |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251001014304/https://highlandecho.com/the-civic-scoop-cornell-on-the-ongoing-tennessee-governors-race/ |archive-date=October 1, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Former [[Lipscomb University]] professor Lauren Pinkston and businessman David Hatley ran as independents.<ref name="Pinkston">{{Cite web |last=Rayner |first=Ruby |date=November 21, 2025 |title=Lauren Pinkston, an independent running for Tennessee governor, wants to give people a third option |url=https://www.timesfreepress.com/news/2025/nov/21/former-lipscomb-university-professor-launches-bid/ |access-date=November 24, 2025 |website=[[Chattanooga Times Free Press]] |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251121202250/https://www.timesfreepress.com/news/2025/nov/21/former-lipscomb-university-professor-launches-bid/ |archive-date=November 21, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Rafferty|first1=Colin|title=Elizabethton-born entrepreneur announces candidacy for governor|url=https://www.wjhl.com/news/your-local-election-hq/elizabethton-born-entrepreneur-announces-candidacy-for-governor/|access-date=March 11, 2026|work=[[WJHL]]|date=March 10, 2026}}</ref>

Blackburn won the election by about 2.5% over Green, a surprisingly narrow margin.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Friedman |first=Adam |date=2026-11-09 |title=The collapse of GOP voters for Blackburn in TN. Two years later, and 1M fewer votes. • Tennessee Lookout |url=https://tennesseelookout.com/2026/11/09/the-collapse-of-gop-voters-for-blackburn-in-tn-two-years-later-and-1m-fewer-votes/ |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=Tennessee Lookout |language=en-US}}</ref> Hatley took over 9% of the vote, attracting support from conservative voters who were dissatisfied with Blackburn.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Smith|first=Chris|date=2026-11-05|title=Trump voters stayed home, Montgomery County swings blue: 4 takeaways on election {{!}} ANALYSIS - ClarksvilleNow.com|url=https://clarksvillenow.com/local/trump-voters-stayed-home-montgomery-county-swings-blue-4-takeaways-on-election-analysis/|access-date=2026-11-07|language=en-US}}</ref> Blackburn became the first woman elected governor of Tennessee and the first candidate since [[1932 Tennessee gubernatorial election|1932]] to win the governorship with less than 50% of the popular vote, securing the smallest margin of victory in a Tennessee gubernatorial election in more than a century. Her victory extended Republican control of the governorship, which the party has held since 2011.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jarrett|first=Kim|date=2026-08-06|title=Tennessee poised to have its first female governor|url=https://www.themountaineer.com/news/national/tennessee-poised-to-have-its-first-female-governor/article_a34121c2-26dc-503a-8fac-d4ae12051c66.html|access-date=2026-08-13|website=The Mountaineer|language=en}}</ref>
