{{Main|2026 Texas gubernatorial election}}

{{See also|2026 Texas lieutenant gubernatorial election}}
Governor [[Greg Abbott]], a Republican, was re-elected in [[2022 Texas gubernatorial election|2022]] with 54.8% of the vote. Because [[Texas]] does not have gubernatorial term limits in its [[Constitution of Texas|Constitution]], he is eligible to run for re-election to a fourth term. On March 1, 2024, Abbott announced his intention to seek re-election.<ref name=absolutely>{{cite web |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2025/11/09/texas-greg-abbott-reelection-campaign-governor/ |title=Gov. Greg Abbott launches reelection campaign for fourth term |date=November 9, 2025 |access-date=November 9, 2025 |publisher=[[The Texas Tribune]] |last=Gou |first=Kayla |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251110013130/https://www.texastribune.org/2025/11/09/texas-greg-abbott-reelection-campaign-governor/ |archive-date=November 10, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He won the primary with over 80% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McGaughy |first1=Lauren |title=Greg Abbott's Primary Win Sets the Stage for a Record Fourth Term as Texas Governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/04/us/elections/abbott-texas-politics.html |access-date=4 March 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=March 4, 2026}}</ref>

The Democratic nominee is state representative [[Gina Hinojosa]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wermund |first1=Benjamin |title=Greg Abbott to face Democrat Gina Hinojosa in bid for a record fourth term as Texas governor |url=https://www.houstonchronicle.com/politics/election/2026/article/governor-primary-results-21329304.php |access-date=4 March 2026 |work=Houston Chronicle |date=March 3, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260304172642/https://www.houstonchronicle.com/politics/election/2026/article/governor-primary-results-21329304.php |archive-date=March 4, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Hinojosa beat [[Chris Bell (politician)|Chris Bell]], a former U.S. representative for [[Texas's 25th congressional district]] and former candidate for governor in [[2006 Texas gubernatorial election|2006]], [[mayor of Houston]] in [[2001 Houston mayoral election|2001]] and [[2015 Houston mayoral election|2015]], and U.S. Senate in [[2020 United States Senate election in Texas|2020]], and Bobby Cole, a rancher, in the primary.<ref name="BellTX">{{Cite news |title=Another candidate joins race for Texas governor |url=https://www.kvue.com/video/news/politics/vote-texas/race-texas-governor-chris-bell/269-d5eefaba-5a93-4566-9d9c-ddb9b3fa93c3 |date=November 10, 2025 |access-date=November 11, 2025 |work=KVUE}}</ref><ref name="HinojosaTX">{{cite web |last1=McKim |first1=Dylan |title=State Rep. Gina Hinojosa enters race for Texas governor |url=https://www.kxan.com/news/texas-politics/state-rep-gina-hinojosa-enters-race-for-texas-governor/ |access-date=October 15, 2025 |work=[[KXAN Austin]] |date=October 15, 2025}}</ref><ref name="ColeTX">{{Cite news |last=Bugenhagen |first=Faith |title=Can an East Texas farmer upset Greg Abbott for governor? |url=https://www.chron.com//politics/article/texas-governor-election-2026-20392211.php |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250627100559/https://www.chron.com//politics/article/texas-governor-election-2026-20392211.php |archive-date=June 27, 2025 |access-date=July 23, 2025 |work=Chron |language=en}}</ref>

Hinojosa defeated Abbott by about seven points, becoming the first Democrat to win a gubernatorial election in Texas since [[Ann Richards]] in [[1990 Texas gubernatorial election|1990]], while Abbott is the first incumbent governor of Texas to lose reelection since Richards' reelection bid in [[1994 Texas gubernatorial election|1994]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Langford |first=Terri |last2=Guo |first2=Kayla |last3=Goldenstein |first3=Taylor |date=2026-11-04 |title=Democrat Gina Hinojosa defeats Greg Abbott for Texas governor |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2026/11/03/texas-governor-greg-abbott-gina-hinojosa-election/ |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US}}</ref>
