{{Main|2026 Vermont gubernatorial election}}

{{See also|2026 Vermont lieutenant gubernatorial election}}

Governor [[Phil Scott]], a Republican, was re-elected in [[2024 Vermont gubernatorial election|2024]] with 73.4% of the vote. Scott ran for a sixth two-year term, declaring only hours before the filing deadline was required. He won the primary unopposed.<ref>{{Cite web |author1=Charlotte Oliver |author2=Shaun Robinson |date=2026-05-28 |title=Gov. Phil Scott is running for reelection |url=https://vtdigger.org/2026/05/28/gov-phil-scott-is-running-for-reelection/ |access-date=2026-05-28 |website=VTDigger |language=en-US}}</ref>

For the Democrats, economist Amanda Janoo served as the nominee.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCallum |first1=Kevin |title=Amanda Janoo Announces Bid for Vermont Governor |url=https://www.sevendaysvt.com/news/amanda-janoo-announces-bid-for-vermont-governor/ |access-date=March 10, 2026 |work=[[Seven Days (newspaper)|Seven Days]] |date=March 10, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260310101326/https://www.sevendaysvt.com/news/amanda-janoo-announces-bid-for-vermont-governor/ |archive-date=March 10, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> She narrowly defeated former lobbying group CEO Aly Richards in the primary. [[Vermont Attorney General|Attorney General]] [[Charity Clark]] was considered a potential candidate and had previously said she that she would run for statewide office in 2026, but she instead decided to seek re-election to her current office.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robinson |first=Shaun |date=2025-08-14 |title=Vermont Lt. Gov. John Rodgers, Secretary of State Sarah Copeland Hanzas say they're running for reelection |url=https://vtdigger.org/2025/08/14/vermont-lt-gov-john-rodgers-secretary-of-state-sarah-copeland-hanzas-say-theyre-running-for-reelection/ |access-date=2025-11-27 |website=[[VTDigger]] |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260105172648/https://vtdigger.org/2025/08/14/vermont-lt-gov-john-rodgers-secretary-of-state-sarah-copeland-hanzas-say-theyre-running-for-reelection/ |archive-date=January 5, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Vermont State Treasurer|State Treasurer]] [[Mike Pieciak]] was reported to have considered a campaign for the governor, but he too choose to seek re-election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robinson |first=Shaun |date=2025-10-14 |title=Vermont Treasurer Mike Pieciak hires top staffer from Rep. Becca Balint |url=https://vtdigger.org/2025/10/14/vermont-treasurer-mike-pieciak-hires-top-staffer-from-rep-becca-balint/ |access-date=2025-11-27 |website=[[VTDigger]] |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251014230443/https://vtdigger.org/2025/10/14/vermont-treasurer-mike-pieciak-hires-top-staffer-from-rep-becca-balint/ |archive-date=October 14, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Oliver |first=Charlotte |date=2026-05-28 |title=Mike Pieciak runs again for treasurer — not governor |url=https://vtdigger.org/2026/05/28/mike-pieciak-runs-again-for-treasurer-not-governor/ |access-date=2026-05-28 |website=VTDigger |language=en-US}}</ref>

[[Dean Roy]], a 14-year-old from [[Stowe, Vermont|Stowe]], ran as a member of the Freedom and Unity Party.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 20, 2025 |title=Stowe teen preps for gubernatorial race |url=https://vtdigger.org/2025/07/20/stowe-teen-preps-for-gubernatorial-race/ |access-date=July 27, 2025 |website=[[VTDigger]] |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250724185558/https://vtdigger.org/2025/07/20/stowe-teen-preps-for-gubernatorial-race/ |archive-date=July 24, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Vermont has no minimum age requirement for the governorship, allowing Roy to seek the governorship despite not being old enough to vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2018-08-13 |title=14-year-old boy uses legal quirk to run for Vermont governor |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/14-year-old-boy-uses-legal-quirk-run-vermont-governor-n900261 |access-date=2025-09-26 |website=[[NBC News]] |language=en}}</ref>

Scott won re-election to a sixth term by just under seven points, the narrowest victory in any of his gubernatorial campaigns.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hirschfeld |first=Peter |date=2026-11-04 |title=Phil Scott wins historic sixth term as Vermont governor |url=https://www.vermontpublic.org/local-news/2026-11-03/phil-scott-wins-historic-sixth-term-as-vermont-governor |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=Vermont Public |language=en}}</ref>
