{{Main|2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election}}

Governor [[Tony Evers]], a Democrat, was re-elected in [[2022 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|2022]] with 51.2% of the vote. Despite [[Wisconsin]] not having gubernatorial term limits in its [[Constitution of Wisconsin|Constitution]], Evers announced on July 24, 2025, that he would not seek re-election.<ref name="Evers">{{cite news |title=Tony Evers will not seek a third term as governor of Wisconsin |url=https://www.tmj4.com/news/state-capitol/tony-evers-will-not-seek-a-third-term-as-governor-of-wisconsin |access-date=July 24, 2025 |work=[[WTMJ-TV]] |date=July 24, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

Milwaukee County executive [[David Crowley]] won the Democratic primary, defeating former secretary of the Wisconsin Department of Administration [[Joel Brennan]], state senator [[Kelda Roys]], and state representative [[Francesca Hong]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Van Wagtendonk |first=Anya |last2=Johnson |first2=Shawn |last3=Casey |first3=Evan |last4=Kremer |first4=Rich |date=2026-08-12 |title=In shocking comeback, David Crowley wins Democratic primary for governor |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/wisconsin-democratic-primary-governor-too-close-to-call-david-crowley-francesca-hong |access-date=2026-11-07 |website=WPR |language=en-US}}</ref>

U.S. Representative for [[Wisconsin's 7th congressional district]] [[Tom Tiffany]] won the Republican primary, defeating medical service technician Andy Manske.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |last2= |date=2026-08-11 |title=With Donald Trump's backing, Tom Tiffany coasts to GOP primary win |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/with-donald-trumps-backing-tom-tiffany-coasts-to-gop-primary-win |access-date=2026-11-07 |website=WPR |language=en-US}}</ref>

Crowley defeated Tiffany by about eight percentage points, becoming Wisconsin's first African American governor.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Herken |first=Olivia |last2=Gretzinger |first2=Erin |date=November 4, 2026 |title=David Crowley wins, will become Wisconsin's first Black governor |url=https://captimes.com/news/elections/david-crowley-wins-will-become-wisconsins-first-black-governor/article_9917edb8-6be8-495f-9fef-aff8f4336cca.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261109074006/https://captimes.com/news/elections/david-crowley-wins-will-become-wisconsins-first-black-governor/article_9917edb8-6be8-495f-9fef-aff8f4336cca.html |archive-date=2026-11-09 |access-date=2026-11-14 |work=The Cap Times |language=en}}</ref> Crowley's eight-point margin of victory is the largest in a Wisconsin gubernatorial election since Republican [[Tommy Thompson]]'s third re-election in [[1998 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|1998]], and the largest margin for a Democrat since Thompson's predecessor, [[Tony Earl]], won election with 57% of the vote in [[1982 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|1982]].
