{{Main|2026 Wyoming gubernatorial election}}

Governor [[Mark Gordon]], a Republican, was re-elected in [[2022 Wyoming gubernatorial election|2022]] with 74.1% of the vote. He was term-limited by state law in 2026 and therefore could not seek reelection to a third consecutive term.

State senator [[Eric Barlow]]<ref name="Barlow">{{cite news |last1=Hill |first1=Alex |title=Wyoming Senator Eric Barlow announces 2026 gubernatorial campaign |url=https://www.yourwyominglink.com/news/politics/wyoming-senator-eric-barlow-announces-2026-gubernatorial-campaign/article_932d7583-9c74-4bc4-80fb-5fde1c577234.html |access-date=August 13, 2025 |work=Your Wyoming Link |date=August 13, 2025 |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250814115916/https://www.yourwyominglink.com/news/politics/wyoming-senator-eric-barlow-announces-2026-gubernatorial-campaign/article_932d7583-9c74-4bc4-80fb-5fde1c577234.html |archive-date=August 14, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> defeated [[Wyoming Department of Education|Wyoming Superintendent of Public Instruction]] [[Megan Degenfelder]]<ref>{{Cite web |author=Noah Zahn |date=2026-01-12 |title=Degenfelder announces bid for governor after Trump endorsement |url=https://www.wyomingnews.com/news/local_news/degenfelder-announces-bid-for-governor-after-trump-endorsement/article_f5ec3cdb-7271-4c54-b1b1-a9ee8e6b17fd.html |access-date=2026-03-26 |website=Wyoming Tribune Eagle |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260420051224/https://www.wyomingnews.com/news/local_news/degenfelder-announces-bid-for-governor-after-trump-endorsement/article_f5ec3cdb-7271-4c54-b1b1-a9ee8e6b17fd.html |archive-date=April 20, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> and civil engineer Brent Bien<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |url=https://cowboystatedaily.com/2024/11/16/republican-brent-bien-first-to-announce-campaign-for-wyoming-governor-in-2026/ |title=Republican Brent Bien First to Announce Campaign for Wyoming Governor in 2026 |access-date=November 26, 2024 |archive-date=November 17, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241117131502/https://cowboystatedaily.com/2024/11/16/republican-brent-bien-first-to-announce-campaign-for-wyoming-governor-in-2026/ |url-status=live }}</ref> for the Republican nomination. Barlow won the general election by a margin of over 50 percentage points.<ref>{{cite web|title=Statewide Candidates Official Summary — Wyoming General Election|publisher=[[Secretary of State of Wyoming]]|date=November 3, 2026|page=3|url=https://sos.wyo.gov/Elections/Docs/2026/Results/General/2026_Wyoming_General_Election_Results.pdf#page=29%22%3E}}</ref>

Perennial candidate Kenneth Casner was the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite web |author1=Wyoming Secretary of State |author1-link=Secretary of State of Wyoming |title=2026 Primary Election Candidate Roster |url=https://sos.wyo.gov/Elections/Docs/2026/2026_WY_Primary_Election_Candidates.pdf |access-date=16 June 2026 |date=15 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261001234123/https://sos.wyo.gov/Elections/Docs/2026/2026_WY_Primary_Election_Candidates.pdf |archive-date=October 1, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Barlow won his first term with 68% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mullen |first=Maggie |date=2026-11-04 |title=Republican Eric Barlow wins Wyoming governor's race |url=https://wyofile.com/republican-eric-barlow-wins-wyoming-governors-race/ |access-date=2026-11-14 |website=WyoFile |language=en-US}}</ref> Casner finished behind [[Constitution Party (United States)|Constitution Party]] nominee Rebecca Bextel, who received 16.6% of the vote to Casner's 14.5%. Casner was the first Democratic gubernatorial nominee since statehood who did not finish in the top two in a Wyoming gubernatorial election, and was the worst-performing of all 36 Democratic gubernatorial nominees in the 2026 cycle.
