{{Main|2026 Guam gubernatorial election}}

Governor [[Lou Leon Guerrero]] was re-elected in [[2022 Guamanian gubernatorial election|2022]] with 55.5% of the vote. She will be term limited in 2026 and cannot seek re-election for a third consecutive term. Lieutenant Governor [[Josh Tenorio]], a Democrat elected alongside Leon Guerrero, announced in 2023 that he would pursue the governorship in 2026.<ref name=tenorio>{{Cite web |last=Eugenio Gilbert |first=Haidee |date=December 3, 2023 |title=Tenorio announces 2026 gubernatorial run |url=https://www.guampdn.com/news/tenorio-announces-2026-gubernatorial-run/article_a4a4a6b6-9187-11ee-891c-6f3ec421f63a.html |access-date=December 31, 2023 |website=Pacific Daily News |language=en}}</ref> Tenorio, however, lost the Democratic primary to [[Therese Terlaje]].

[[Republican Party of Guam|Republican]] nominee [[Vicente Ada]] won the election with 51.5% of the vote, flipping the office from the Democrats by defeating Terlaje who received 48.2% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.guampdn.com/news/1-30-am-unofficial-results-show-ada-calvo-wins-adelup-limtiaco-takes-congress-tydingco-new/article_19c8dff1-86a5-4420-9e35-772c0b8f26d8.html|newspaper=[[Pacific Daily News]]|title=Unofficial results: Ada-Calvo wins Adelup, Limtiaco takes Congress, Tydingco new AG|date=November 4, 2026|author=Joe Taitano II}}</ref> This marked the first time since [[2014 Guamanian gubernatorial election|2014]] that a Republican had been elected as Governor of Guam. Guam was also unique in having the only 2026 gubernatorial race that the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] gained from the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] in what was otherwise dubbed a "[[Blue wave of 2026|blue wave]]" nationwide.
