The '''2026 Hawaii gubernatorial election''' was held on November 3, 2026, to elect the [[governor of Hawaii]]. [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] incumbent [[Josh Green (politician)|Josh Green]] defeated Republican businessman Gary Cordery to win a second term in office.<ref name=":1">{{cite web|date=May 14, 2026|title=2026 Candidate Report|url=https://olvr.hawaii.gov/Controls/CandidateFiling.aspx?elid=94|access-date=June 1, 2026|website=[[Hawaii Secretary of State]]}}</ref>

[[Primary elections]] took place on August 8, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web|author=The Sunshine Blog|date=May 9, 2025|title=2026 State Primary Election Dates|url=https://www.ncsl.org/elections-and-campaigns/2026-state-primary-election-dates|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260523194629/https://www.ncsl.org/elections-and-campaigns/2026-state-primary-election-dates|archive-date=May 23, 2026|url-status=live|website=[[National Conference of State Legislatures]]|access-date=August 5, 2025}}</ref> Green secured the Democratic nomination with 88.5% of the vote against three other candidates. Cordery defeated Ken Fujiyama in the Republican primary with 75.9% of the vote.

Green received the highest vote share of any gubernatorial candidate in Hawaii history, winning nearly 73 percent of the vote and defeating his opponent by over 45 points.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Budiono |first1=Victoria |title=Gov. Josh Green secures 2nd term in Hawaii’s largest gubernatorial victory ever |url=https://www.staradvertiser.com/2026/11/04/hawaii-news/gov-josh-green-secures-2nd-term-in-hawaiis-largest-gubernatorial-victory-ever/ |website=Honolulu Star-Advertiser |access-date=7 November 2026 |language=en |date=4 November 2026}}</ref>

Republicans have not won a gubernatorial election in Hawaii since [[2006 Hawaii gubernatorial election|2006]].

